{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790536587,"nonce":"/PSFb/7DM1LYsJtNWRW78uXsav8=","message":"spectral_norm, second round (branch perf/jit-spectral-norm-loop, c959a71..b0cc6a3 on 6fb7c5a). Cloud box, x86_64, 4 cores; noise here is about +-15%, so only large effects count.\n\nLanded:\n- d0fc449: Cranelift had no inline List store at all; every `list[i] = x` called wren_subscript_set. Num stores into a List are now inline (elem_class set to mixed, as ObjList::set does). Cranelift-only build: fannkuch_redux 2232 -\u003e 1306 ms, reverse_complement 27.9 -\u003e 22.9 ms. The shipped llvm build: fannkuch unchanged (1130 ms both), it runs on the LLVM tier.\n- d0fc449 also skips the receiver checks of a read/store whose receiver a dominating GuardClassAt(List) proves; 46328a2 makes value_roots see a receiver carried around two nested loops (back edges passing a copy of the parameter).\n- 929840e: IntSpecialize widened a parameter with one source (the inner loop's copy of the outer counter) to +-2^53 while the source still grew, so `i + j` stayed f64. Now integer.\n- b0cc6a3: installing the optimised body replaced the published OSR table with its own entries; a loop it had no entry for then had none at all, and an interpreter frame in that loop stayed interpreted for the rest of the call (seen as a 116 ms first round when the early top tier is enabled for all functions). Baseline entries stay published for loops the optimised body lacks.\n\nWhere the time goes (10-round kernel, Cranelift tier, ~45 ms): warm-up ~14 ms, then ~3.4 ms per round steady state (~31 ms). LuaJIT: ~12 ms total, ~1.1 ms per round. Steady state dominates; warm-up alone cannot close it. The interpreter runs this loop at ~320 ns/iteration.\n\nTried and backed out:\n1. Early top tier for every function (queue Optimized beside Baseline from the interpreter's profile): 10 rounds 45 -\u003e 62 ms; the early body is compiled before denominator has code and without the list-read profile.\n2. Optimised bodies have no OSR entry at integer-bounded loops: the bound is f64_to_i64(unbox count) in a preheader, not a register and not rematerialisable, so osr_entry_layout rejects it (check #2, external type). Implemented recomputing pure conversions (Unbox/Box/Move/F64ToI64/I64ToF64/class tests) at the entry from received values, with the natural loop (not everything the header reaches) as the region. Entries then exist, but no measurable gain, and compile time went up (live_in_sets/infer_value_types per call per header); mandelbrot slower.\n3. Forwarding carried loop parameters to their original values (dropping the header parameters) + LICM: the MIR inner loop gets lean (object_is on this, i-\u003ef64 hoisted; header params only j, sum). Net slower across the suite: mandelbrot +60%, reverse_complement +70%, fannkuch +25%, spectral_norm no gain. Cause: every GuardNumAt/SlowPathExit deopt state lists the interpreter frame (this, v, out, n, i, ...), ~10 loop-invariant values whose only uses are cold; regalloc2 keeps them live and shuffles them between registers and spill slots on the hot path every iteration. Rebuilding constants and boxes inside the deopt block did not change it.\n   This also exposed that Licm will hoist SubscriptGet / GetField / ListCount and boxed operators on non-Num values out of a loop that writes the same memory or runs code (fannkuch n=9 looped forever). Unreachable on main: receivers in loops are loop parameters, and a loop with a SubscriptGet counts as running code for module variables.\n\nConclusion for the Cranelift tier: its steady state is bounded by deopt-state pressure, not by the instructions in the loop body. Options:\n- Smaller deopt states: resume the interpreter at the loop header rather than mid-body, or rebuild frame values from fewer roots, so a guard keeps 3-4 values live instead of ~12.\n- Reach the LLVM tier's steady state sooner: LLVM's loop is ~2 ms/round (vs 3.4) because LLVM spills cold-only values without hot-path moves; it installs at ~35 ms even when queued at once (codegen 11-42 ms per function).","files":null}]}